1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:13,011
On the morning of the twenty-seventh of May, 1942, an open-topped Mercedes slowed at a sharp bend in a Prague suburb. Two men were waiting by the tram stop.

2
00:00:14,111 --> 00:00:23,284
In the car sat one of the most feared men in occupied Europe. He was Heinrich Himmler's right-hand man and the head of the Nazi police state.

3
00:00:24,384 --> 00:00:35,221
Four months earlier, he had chaired the meeting at Wannsee, near Berlin, where the murder of Europe's Jews was coordinated. His office counted eleven million of them.

4
00:00:36,321 --> 00:00:39,884
By the end of the war, about six million had been killed.

5
00:00:40,984 --> 00:00:51,864
Reinhard Heydrich was not the face of the Nazi regime. He was one of its engineers: cold, ambitious and very efficient. This is how he built the machine.

6
00:00:54,064 --> 00:01:07,099
Reinhard Heydrich was born on the seventh of March, 1904, in Halle, in central Germany. His father, Bruno, was an opera singer and composer who ran a music school.

7
00:01:08,199 --> 00:01:21,852
He grew up a gifted violinist and a ferocious competitor at sport, especially fencing. After Germany's defeat in the First World War, as a teenager, he drifted into right-wing nationalist groups.

8
00:01:22,952 --> 00:01:32,275
In 1922 he joined the navy as a cadet and became a signals officer. He was ambitious, and widely disliked.

9
00:01:33,375 --> 00:01:48,991
In 1931 his career ended in scandal. A young woman said he had promised to marry her, but he became engaged to someone else. A naval court of honour judged his conduct unworthy of an officer, and he was dismissed.

10
00:01:50,091 --> 00:02:01,269
His new fiancée, Lina von Osten, was already a committed Nazi. She urged him to try a small party organisation that was looking for talent: the SS.

11
00:02:03,469 --> 00:02:14,328
In June 1931 he met Heinrich Himmler, the head of the SS. Himmler asked him to sketch out a plan for an intelligence service. He was hired.

12
00:02:15,428 --> 00:02:25,241
That plan became the SD, the party's own security service. Its job was to spy on the Nazis' enemies, and on fellow Nazis too.

13
00:02:26,341 --> 00:02:38,053
After Hitler took power in 1933, Heydrich rose fast. In 1934 he took charge of the Gestapo, the secret state police.

14
00:02:39,153 --> 00:02:48,967
That summer, he helped prepare the lists for the Night of the Long Knives, when Hitler had the leaders of his own street army, the SA, and other rivals murdered.

15
00:02:50,067 --> 00:03:01,352
Under Heydrich, the Gestapo could seize people without trial and send them to concentration camps. Communists, socialists, priests, gay men and Jews filled them.

16
00:03:03,552 --> 00:03:20,149
During Kristallnacht, in November 1938, Heydrich sent urgent orders to police across Germany. They were not to stop the attacks on synagogues and Jewish shops, and they were to arrest as many Jewish men as the prisons could hold.

17
00:03:21,249 --> 00:03:36,012
On the last night of August 1939, his men staged a fake Polish attack on a German radio station at Gleiwitz. The next morning, Germany invaded Poland, pointing to incidents like this one.

18
00:03:37,112 --> 00:03:49,336
That September, his police and intelligence agencies were merged into one office: the Reich Main Security Office. Heydrich ran it. It became the headquarters of Nazi terror.

19
00:03:50,436 --> 00:04:02,084
In Poland, special units under his command, the Einsatzgruppen, followed the army. They shot tens of thousands of civilians, among them teachers, priests, officials and Jews.

20
00:04:04,284 --> 00:04:16,380
In June 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union. Behind the troops came four Einsatzgruppen, about three thousand men, reporting to Heydrich's office.

21
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:27,699
Town by town, they rounded up Jewish men, then women and children, and shot them outside town. Local police and German army units often helped.

22
00:04:28,799 --> 00:04:40,575
Historians estimate that the killing squads and their helpers shot more than a million people, most of them Jews. Heydrich's office received their reports, which counted the dead.

23
00:04:41,675 --> 00:04:55,925
On the thirty-first of July, 1941, Hermann Göring, Hitler's deputy, signed a letter instructing Heydrich to prepare what it called a total solution of the Jewish question in Europe.

24
00:04:58,125 --> 00:05:10,221
In September 1941, Hitler sent him to Prague as Deputy Reich Protector of Bohemia and Moravia, the occupied Czech lands. In practice, he was their ruler.

25
00:05:11,321 --> 00:05:22,692
He declared martial law the day after he arrived. Within weeks, hundreds of people had been sentenced to death and thousands more arrested. Czechs called him the Butcher of Prague.

26
00:05:23,792 --> 00:05:31,323
Resistance networks were rounded up one by one, and the executions announced on posters across the city.

27
00:05:32,423 --> 00:05:43,879
At the same time, he raised food rations and pensions for Czech workers. The aim was simple: keep the arms factories running for Germany, and crush everyone who resisted.

28
00:05:46,079 --> 00:05:57,321
On the twentieth of January, 1942, Heydrich and fourteen other senior officials met at a lakeside villa at Wannsee, on the edge of Berlin.

29
00:05:58,421 --> 00:06:06,549
Around the table sat men from government ministries, the Nazi party and the SS. The meeting took about ninety minutes.

30
00:06:07,649 --> 00:06:20,940
The minutes, written by Adolf Eichmann, one of Heydrich's officers, listed eleven million Jews across Europe. They even counted Jews in countries Germany had not conquered, such as Britain and Ireland.

31
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:33,155
Wannsee did not start the Holocaust. The shootings and the first gassings had already begun. Its purpose was to bring the whole state into the plan, under Heydrich's lead.

32
00:06:35,355 --> 00:06:53,168
In London, the Czechoslovak government in exile had a plan. British special forces trained two Czechoslovak soldiers, Jozef Gabčík, a Slovak, and Jan Kubiš, a Czech. In December 1941 they parachuted into the occupied country.

33
00:06:54,268 --> 00:07:03,847
On the twenty-seventh of May, Heydrich was driven to work without an armed escort. At the bend, Gabčík stepped out and aimed his gun. It jammed.

34
00:07:04,947 --> 00:07:18,472
Kubiš threw a bomb. It exploded beside the car, and both attackers escaped. Heydrich was badly wounded. He seemed to be recovering in hospital, but infection set in, and he died on the fourth of June.

35
00:07:19,572 --> 00:07:24,649
At his state funeral in Berlin, Hitler called him the man with the iron heart.

36
00:07:26,849 --> 00:07:39,820
Hitler demanded revenge. The village of Lidice was falsely linked to the attackers. On the tenth of June, its men, one hundred and seventy-three of them, were shot. The village was burned and levelled.

37
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:52,333
Its women were sent to concentration camps. Most of its children were murdered at Chełmno, in occupied Poland. Two weeks later, the village of Ležáky was destroyed too.

38
00:07:53,433 --> 00:08:07,023
The parachutists hid in the crypt of a Prague church. A fellow parachutist, Karel Čurda, betrayed them for a reward. On the eighteenth of June, after hours of fighting, all seven men in the church were dead.

39
00:08:08,123 --> 00:08:14,565
By most estimates, well over a thousand Czechs were killed in the reprisals for Heydrich's death.

40
00:08:16,765 --> 00:08:27,411
The machine he built outlived him. The death camps of Belzec, Sobibor and Treblinka were part of a programme the SS named after him: Operation Reinhard.

41
00:08:28,511 --> 00:08:36,212
According to the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum, about one point seven million Jews were murdered in it.

42
00:08:37,312 --> 00:08:53,653
Lidice became a symbol around the world. Towns in the Americas took its name, and miners in Stoke-on-Trent raised money to rebuild it. Today a rose garden and a memorial to its eighty-two murdered children stand near where the village was.

43
00:08:54,753 --> 00:09:09,857
Heydrich rarely pulled a trigger. His tools were offices, card files, telegrams and meetings. That is the lesson of his life: genocide needs managers as well as killers, and Heydrich was one of the most effective it ever had.
